WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:05.480
Did you know the global average cost of a breach is $4.42M USD?

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:11.130
And it takes on average 287 days to identify and contain a breach?

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:13.756
The problem is many of the tools in the marketplace
today

00:00:13.756 --> 00:00:16.295
fail to adequately stop and prevent breaches.

00:00:16.442 --> 00:00:19.173
Hi, my name is Tim Brantner and I'm with IBM Cloud.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:23.779
Today I have the honor of talking to you about
the IBM Cloud Security and Compliance Center

00:00:23.779 --> 00:00:27.000
and how it can help you solve this problem in one view.

00:00:27.413 --> 00:00:30.635
As customers encounter moving regulated workloads
to the cloud,

00:00:30.635 --> 00:00:33.516
they have to keep several things in mind and achieve.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:36.480
They want to manage risk and compliance

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:39.518
with as much automation and standardization as possible

00:00:39.518 --> 00:00:42.328
to increase effectiveness and lower costs.

00:00:42.621 --> 00:00:45.943
They want to ensure data protection with concepts
like zero trust

00:00:45.943 --> 00:00:49.438
by implementing least privilege for their identity access management,

00:00:49.438 --> 00:00:51.345
or ensuring storage is encrypted.

00:00:51.999 --> 00:00:56.668
They want to automate continuous detection and response for their misconfiguration of cloud resources

00:00:56.895 --> 00:01:01.034
to reduce the probability of being the next big data breach in the news.

00:01:01.247 --> 00:01:06.879
They want to make it simple to infuse security
and privacy for their cloud developers with DevSecOps.

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.658
But before we get into IBM Cloud Security and Compliance Center (SCC),

00:01:10.738 --> 00:01:14.432
first, we have to talk about CSPM, or cloud security posture management.

00:01:14.672 --> 00:01:16.758
It is a category of security products

00:01:16.758 --> 00:01:21.445
designed to identify misconfigurations and compliance risks in the cloud.

00:01:21.752 --> 00:01:26.723
CSPM tools work by examining a cloud environment
against a defined set of best practices

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.722
and known security risks.

00:01:29.072 --> 00:01:31.984
It helps cloud applications be more secure

00:01:31.984 --> 00:01:36.290
and ensure compliance with regulatory or corporate compliance standards.

00:01:36.423 --> 00:01:41.455
We have a video entirely on the CSPM subject
done by one of our great team members, Janet Van.

00:01:41.749 --> 00:01:42.989
What is SCC?

00:01:43.069 --> 00:01:48.380
SCC is IBM Cloud's answer for simplified and
centralized security compliance posture.

00:01:48.714 --> 00:01:53.780
It is geared for enterprises with regulated
workloads needing to accelerate public cloud adoption.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.035
We want your workloads to be secure by default

00:01:57.035 --> 00:01:59.059
with the easiest onboarding experience.

00:01:59.206 --> 00:02:02.598
That's why we made SCC part of the IBM Cloud platform.

00:02:02.731 --> 00:02:06.774
No installing, no instances to provision, just there by default.

00:02:06.867 --> 00:02:09.000
And with every IBM Cloud account.

00:02:09.150 --> 00:02:11.562
From there, it's simple to start seeing results.

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:16.565
IBM Cloud is the only cloud service provider that has the ability to scan other clouds.

00:02:16.739 --> 00:02:19.740
The only one that has depth in IBM Cloud,

00:02:19.740 --> 00:02:24.306
and continue to expand our hybrid multi-cloud capabilities with IBM Cloud Satellite.

00:02:24.733 --> 00:02:29.875
SCC is the central way to proactively enforce
or prevent misconfiguration

00:02:29.875 --> 00:02:32.026
for all IBM Cloud services.

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:36.507
All this while providing the best level of production experience:

00:02:36.507 --> 00:02:39.756
stable, reliable, scalable and extensible

00:02:39.756 --> 00:02:42.000
through APIs and Terraform templates.

00:02:42.209 --> 00:02:47.569
As well as integrations with cool leading partners like Tanium, Caveonix, NeuVector,

00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:50.010
Twistlock and our OpenShift compliance operator.

00:02:50.904 --> 00:02:52.074
So, let's dig in.

00:02:52.314 --> 00:02:54.636
First, how do I access SCC?

00:02:54.970 --> 00:02:58.788
Well, it's as easy as logging into IBM Cloud on ibm.com

00:02:58.788 --> 00:03:02.510
and go into the left-hand hamburger menu to find "Security and Compliance".

00:03:02.603 --> 00:03:03.812
Or, in this example,

00:03:03.812 --> 00:03:07.190
you can click on the Security and Compliance widget that gives you a quick snapshot

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:10.320
of your security score and ops detractors.

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:14.584
Or, you can click "Manage" in the top nav and select "Security and Compliance".

00:03:14.584 --> 00:03:18.552
Once you get into the SCC experience, there's several ways to get started.

00:03:18.552 --> 00:03:23.302
We have several examples, videos and links directly to documentation.

00:03:23.302 --> 00:03:27.606
And besides, we're constantly making onboarding experience easier and easier.

00:03:27.606 --> 00:03:30.566
When monitoring for configuration compliance,

00:03:30.566 --> 00:03:35.404
you'll start off by selecting a pre-defined profile or creating a profile of your own.

00:03:35.404 --> 00:03:40.075
A profile is a grouping of control implementations that we call "goals".

00:03:40.075 --> 00:03:42.446
Once you have what you want to check,

00:03:42.446 --> 00:03:46.164
you'll need to identify what resources you want to run those checks on.

00:03:46.164 --> 00:03:48.833
We call those set of resources a "scope".

00:03:48.833 --> 00:03:53.247
Then once that is ready, you'll set up a collector and scan details.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.838
At that point, you have an automated scan providing continuous compliance and your compliance score.

00:03:59.838 --> 00:04:03.793
So let's say you're monitoring, but want to be a little bit more proactive.

00:04:03.793 --> 00:04:07.475
That is where our configuration governance capabilities come into play.

00:04:07.475 --> 00:04:12.000
Configuration governance enables you to define, manage, enforce

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:15.612
and monitor the configuration rules of your IBM Cloud resources.

00:04:15.612 --> 00:04:19.730
It's set up by defining, customizing and enabling those configuration rules,

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:24.283
or you can group them together in what we call "configuration
rule" templates.

00:04:24.458 --> 00:04:29.741
Once you have set up compliance scans, enabled configuration rules, and set security alerts,

00:04:29.845 --> 00:04:31.965
you'll have a nice dashboard -- like this one.

00:04:32.734 --> 00:04:37.252
This one dashboard provides a quick view of all three capability areas of SSC

00:04:37.314 --> 00:04:41.000
and allows  you to manage your security and compliance posture from one place.

00:04:41.594 --> 00:04:43.223
Or, if UI not your thing,

00:04:43.223 --> 00:04:47.726
we have a full list of APIs and CLIs available to interopt with your security tools.

00:04:48.036 --> 00:04:49.134
So there you have it.

00:04:49.414 --> 00:04:52.739
You just had a whirlwind tour of the IBM Cloud Security Compliance Center.

00:04:52.739 --> 00:04:57.638
Honestly, there's so much more value inside
of it that I want you to go check out, but just keep this in mind:

00:04:58.103 --> 00:05:02.419
it's IBM Cloud's answer for centralized security and compliance posture

00:05:02.419 --> 00:05:05.215
for enterprises with regulated workloads

00:05:05.215 --> 00:05:07.705
needing to accelerate public cloud adoption.

00:05:07.705 --> 00:05:10.168
Thanks for watching and hope to catch you in the next video.

